(1727180, 230232, 37707, '<td valign="top">Whenever you''re shooting at the moon, don''t try long exposures. The moon is pretty bright by itself. Ona  full moon, with ISO 400 try 1/250 with f8.<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Bus352:</b></div><hr/><i> I''m still trying to get moon shots as clear as this one, without over exposure--what''s your secret? Slow shutter, opened aperature? Good minimalist capture. </i></td></tr></table></td>', 'Whenever you''re shooting at the moon, don''t try long exposures. The moon is pretty bright by itself. Ona  full moon, with ISO 400 try 1/250 with f8. Originally posted by Bus352: I''m still trying to get moon shots as clear as this one, without over exposure--what''s your secret? Slow shutter, opened aperature? Good minimalist capture. ', '2005-09-21 13:01:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(1727201, 231328, 19670, '<td valign="top">Thanks to all for the kind comments. This ended being my 2nd-highest pic to date. This is a (flipped) shot of one of the buildings (office space) where I work. (Forgot to comment that I also did some sharpening.) I don''t quite know why the ceiling ended up grainy, even after some Neat Image was applied.<br/><i>edit for typo</i><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-11-21 14:03:33.</i></td>', 'Thanks to all for the kind comments. This ended being my 2nd-highest pic to date. This is a (flipped) shot of one of the buildings (office space) where I work. (Forgot to comment that I also did some sharpening.) I don''t quite know why the ceiling ended up grainy, even after some Neat Image was applied.edit for typo', '2005-09-21 13:09:07.000000', '2005-11-21 14:03:33.000000', 0),
